1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,062
Most teams have backups.

2
00:00:02,062 --> 00:00:05,380
Far fewer know that their backups actually restore.

3
00:00:05,933 --> 00:00:11,323
Then one day someone runs drop table orders on the production database, at five past two in the afternoon.

4
00:00:11,867 --> 00:00:17,013
And during the incident the team finds out that the backup job had been failing for weeks,

5
00:00:17,013 --> 00:00:20,215
or the files are corrupt, or the encryption key is gone.

6
00:00:20,215 --> 00:00:24,447
Or the newest backup is from last night, so a whole day of orders is lost.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,191
In this video I will show you a PostgreSQL setup that proves, every single day, that its backups restore.

8
00:00:31,191 --> 00:00:33,608
It archives every change within a minute,

9
00:00:33,608 --> 00:00:38,030
restores a copy every day and checks it, and alerts when anything is wrong.

10
00:00:38,567 --> 00:00:39,856
And it is all measured.

11
00:00:39,856 --> 00:00:42,155
A dropped table is undone in ten seconds,

12
00:00:42,155 --> 00:00:44,958
with all 123 orders from before the accident back,

13
00:00:44,958 --> 00:00:48,771
and a silently damaged backup file is caught before anyone needs it.

14
00:00:48,771 --> 00:00:52,247
The code and a free study guide are linked in the description.

15
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,095
Here is the plan.

16
00:00:55,095 --> 00:01:00,182
First the problem, and the one idea that makes point in time recovery possible:

17
00:01:00,182 --> 00:01:01,470
the write ahead log.

18
00:01:02,033 --> 00:01:06,593
Then the architecture, with three separate networks, and the security design.

19
00:01:07,133 --> 00:01:08,021
Then the tools:

20
00:01:08,021 --> 00:01:13,703
pgBackRest for backups, the daily restore drill, and the monitoring with Prometheus and Grafana.

21
00:01:14,267 --> 00:01:17,314
Next, five real disasters, each one measured:

22
00:01:17,314 --> 00:01:23,545
a dropped table, a storage outage, bit rot, a lost encryption key, and a lost database disk.

23
00:01:23,545 --> 00:01:27,067
And the tests that repeat all of them on every push.

24
00:01:27,633 --> 00:01:34,433
And finally, how to run the whole stack on your own laptop, and how to move it to AWS S3.

25
00:01:36,167 --> 00:01:38,148
Let us start with the problem.

26
00:01:38,148 --> 00:01:39,865
Backup jobs fail silently.

27
00:01:39,865 --> 00:01:45,677
A cron job stops working, and nobody reads the backup logs until the day they need them.

28
00:01:46,233 --> 00:01:48,966
A nightly dump has no point in time recovery.

29
00:01:48,966 --> 00:01:51,759
It cannot undo a delete made at five past two.

30
00:01:51,759 --> 00:01:55,463
You either lose up to a day of data, or you keep the mistake.

31
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,796
Ask a team how long a restore would take, and how much data they would lose.

32
00:02:00,796 --> 00:02:05,340
Those are the RTO and the RPO, and they usually have no measured answer.

33
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:08,741
Often the backups live right next to the database.

34
00:02:08,741 --> 00:02:13,060
If an attacker or ransomware gets the database host, the backups go with it.

35
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:15,591
And storage is never verified.

36
00:02:15,591 --> 00:02:20,570
A damaged file in object storage is only discovered on the day you need it.

37
00:02:22,333 --> 00:02:23,359
Here is the idea.

38
00:02:23,359 --> 00:02:28,908
PostgreSQL writes every change into the write ahead log, the WAL, before it changes a table.

39
00:02:28,908 --> 00:02:30,415
Start with a full backup:

40
00:02:30,415 --> 00:02:31,923
a copy of the data files.

41
00:02:32,467 --> 00:02:37,484
Then archive every piece of WAL to object storage as it is written.

42
00:02:37,484 --> 00:02:41,827
In this project that happens at least every sixty seconds.

43
00:02:42,367 --> 00:02:47,598
Now you can restore the full copy and replay the WAL up to any second you choose.

44
00:02:47,598 --> 00:02:53,605
For example, to four minutes and fifty nine seconds past two, one second before the accident.

45
00:02:53,605 --> 00:02:55,607
That is point in time recovery.

46
00:02:56,167 --> 00:02:58,317
Two numbers describe how good this is.

47
00:02:58,317 --> 00:03:03,127
The RPO, how much data you can lose, is bounded by the sixty second archive interval.

48
00:03:03,127 --> 00:03:08,277
And the RTO, how long a restore takes, is something this project measures every single day.

49
00:03:10,033 --> 00:03:11,518
Here is the architecture.

50
00:03:11,518 --> 00:03:16,863
A small shop application writes orders all the time, so that data loss becomes measurable.

51
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:19,466
The database is PostgreSQL 18.

52
00:03:19,466 --> 00:03:23,941
It archives WAL through a dedicated backup host, over mutual TLS.

53
00:03:23,941 --> 00:03:28,830
The database itself holds no storage keys and no encryption passphrase.

54
00:03:29,367 --> 00:03:34,951
The backup host owns the pgBackRest repository in S3 compatible object storage.

55
00:03:34,951 --> 00:03:39,687
In the lab that is SeaweedFS, and in production you swap in AWS S3.

56
00:03:40,233 --> 00:03:45,064
The backup host also runs the schedule, the verification and the restore drills,

57
00:03:45,064 --> 00:03:47,540
and publishes every result to Prometheus.

58
00:03:47,540 --> 00:03:49,593
Grafana shows it on one dashboard.

59
00:03:50,133 --> 00:03:52,313
And there are three separate networks.

60
00:03:52,313 --> 00:03:56,386
The database and the application are not on the storage network at all.

61
00:03:56,386 --> 00:04:01,433
So even a fully compromised database host cannot read, change or delete its own backups.

62
00:04:03,167 --> 00:04:04,855
This is defence in depth.

63
00:04:04,855 --> 00:04:09,381
First, every backup and WAL file is encrypted at rest with AES 256.

64
00:04:09,381 --> 00:04:13,637
The test searches the raw files for order data, and finds none.

65
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:15,923
Second, separation of duties.

66
00:04:15,923 --> 00:04:22,280
The storage credentials and the encryption passphrase only exist on the backup host, never on the database.

67
00:04:22,833 --> 00:04:25,933
Third, network isolation, as we just saw.

68
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:29,808
And fourth, mutual TLS between the database and the backup host,

69
00:04:29,808 --> 00:04:34,770
with certificates from a private certificate authority whose key is kept out of every container.

70
00:04:36,533 --> 00:04:40,649
The backup tool is pgBackRest, pinned to version 2.59.

71
00:04:40,649 --> 00:04:47,583
It does parallel, encrypted and block incremental backups, with asynchronous WAL archiving.

72
00:04:48,133 --> 00:04:52,588
The database is configured with archive mode on, and an archive command.

73
00:04:52,588 --> 00:04:56,301
For every finished WAL segment, PostgreSQL calls pgBackRest,

74
00:04:56,301 --> 00:05:00,013
which pushes it through the backup host into the repository.

75
00:05:00,567 --> 00:05:08,476
Archive timeout sixty forces a new WAL segment at least every sixty seconds, even when the shop is quiet.

76
00:05:08,476 --> 00:05:11,037
That is what bounds the data loss.

77
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,191
And notice the networks line:

78
00:05:13,191 --> 00:05:15,440
the database is only on the data network.

79
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:19,370
The backup host runs a schedule.

80
00:05:19,370 --> 00:05:25,472
A full backup every Sunday, a differential every other day, and an incremental every hour.

81
00:05:25,472 --> 00:05:30,760
So a restore needs one full, one differential, a few incrementals and the WAL.

82
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:37,256
Every night, verify reads every single file in the repository and checks its checksum.

83
00:05:37,256 --> 00:05:39,680
That is how bit rot is found early.

84
00:05:40,233 --> 00:05:43,723
Then the restore drill, the heart of the project.

85
00:05:44,267 --> 00:05:48,272
Every minute, the repository state is exported for the dashboard.

86
00:05:48,272 --> 00:05:53,817
The scheduler is supercronic, a cron built for containers, pinned by its SHA 256 checksum.

87
00:05:55,567 --> 00:05:57,267
Here is the restore drill.

88
00:05:57,267 --> 00:06:02,629
First, it reads the newest order in the live database, with two read only queries.

89
00:06:02,629 --> 00:06:05,637
That is the point the copy should get back to.

90
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:10,029
Then it restores the latest backup, plus all the archived WAL,

91
00:06:10,029 --> 00:06:13,240
from S3 into a scratch directory on the backup host.

92
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:16,765
It starts that copy as a completely separate PostgreSQL,

93
00:06:16,765 --> 00:06:21,900
on another port, with no network and no WAL archiving, so it can never interfere with production.

94
00:06:22,467 --> 00:06:23,757
Then it checks the copy:

95
00:06:23,757 --> 00:06:28,380
are any orders missing, how far behind the live database is the newest restored order,

96
00:06:28,380 --> 00:06:31,767
and does amcheck find any corruption in the tables and indexes?

97
00:06:32,333 --> 00:06:35,585
Finally it throws the copy away, and publishes the result:

98
00:06:35,585 --> 00:06:40,183
the measured restore time, the measured data loss, the row counts and the verdict.

99
00:06:41,933 --> 00:06:44,436
Here is a real drill from the logs.

100
00:06:44,436 --> 00:06:46,653
It notes the newest live order.

101
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:54,660
pgBackRest restores the latest backup set, 30 megabytes in 1274 files, in under four seconds.

102
00:06:55,200 --> 00:07:02,018
The copy has 111 orders, none missing, and its newest order is only one second older than the live database.

103
00:07:02,018 --> 00:07:04,670
That one second is the measured data loss.

104
00:07:05,233 --> 00:07:07,467
The whole drill passed in six seconds.

105
00:07:07,467 --> 00:07:13,933
On a production size database this takes longer, and an alert fires if it ever takes more than thirty minutes.

106
00:07:15,667 --> 00:07:19,629
Every job publishes its result to a Prometheus Pushgateway,

107
00:07:19,629 --> 00:07:23,794
and a postgres exporter reports the state of the WAL archiver.

108
00:07:23,794 --> 00:07:26,548
The dashboard answers one question first:

109
00:07:26,548 --> 00:07:28,227
can we restore right now?

110
00:07:28,767 --> 00:07:29,461
At the top:

111
00:07:29,461 --> 00:07:33,567
the last drill's result, the measured restore time and data loss,

112
00:07:33,567 --> 00:07:36,599
missing orders, and the repository verification.

113
00:07:36,599 --> 00:07:36,977
Below:

114
00:07:36,977 --> 00:07:43,357
the age of each backup type, firing alerts, the repository size, WAL archiving and the drill history.

115
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:50,176
There are eleven alert rules, one for every way backups can silently stop working.

116
00:07:50,176 --> 00:07:51,786
Two watch the WAL archive:

117
00:07:51,786 --> 00:07:52,900
failing, or stale.

118
00:07:53,467 --> 00:07:54,942
Four watch the backups:

119
00:07:54,942 --> 00:08:01,037
a failed backup, a backup or full backup that is too old, and a repository that cannot be read.

120
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:03,110
One watches integrity:

121
00:08:03,110 --> 00:08:04,620
a failed verification.

122
00:08:05,167 --> 00:08:06,885
And four watch the drills:

123
00:08:06,885 --> 00:08:13,760
a failed drill, a missing drill, a restore slower than thirty minutes, and data loss above five minutes.

124
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:16,537
Every rule has a unit test with prom tool.

125
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:21,976
Now the results, from one clean run of the end to end test.

126
00:08:21,976 --> 00:08:27,210
From an empty machine to a running stack with its first full backup took 68 seconds.

127
00:08:27,767 --> 00:08:32,837
A dropped table was undone in ten seconds, with every order from before the accident back.

128
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:37,300
A storage outage of about three and a half minutes lost zero orders.

129
00:08:37,867 --> 00:08:42,107
A silently damaged backup file was caught by verification and by the drill.

130
00:08:42,667 --> 00:08:47,547
A wrong encryption passphrase made nothing restorable, and the alert said so.

131
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:52,405
And a deleted database volume was rebuilt from the backups in sixteen seconds.

132
00:08:52,405 --> 00:08:53,730
Let us look at each one.

133
00:08:55,467 --> 00:08:56,663
Disaster one:

134
00:08:56,663 --> 00:08:59,516
someone drops the orders table.

135
00:08:59,516 --> 00:09:02,737
At that moment, 123 orders existed.

136
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:09,027
One command, pitr dot S-H, with a target time one second before the accident.

137
00:09:09,027 --> 00:09:16,390
pgBackRest uses a delta restore, so it only replaces the files that differ, in a third of a second.

138
00:09:16,933 --> 00:09:19,659
PostgreSQL replays the WAL up to the target,

139
00:09:19,659 --> 00:09:24,863
and the database is back in service in ten seconds, on a new timeline, timeline two.

140
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:30,807
All 123 orders from before the accident are back, and nothing from after the target.

141
00:09:30,807 --> 00:09:35,120
The app starts writing again, and a new full backup and drill pass.

142
00:09:36,867 --> 00:09:37,729
Disaster two:

143
00:09:37,729 --> 00:09:41,977
the object storage goes down for about three and a half minutes.

144
00:09:41,977 --> 00:09:44,366
The database keeps accepting orders.

145
00:09:44,366 --> 00:09:48,747
WAL archiving fails eight times, and the segments wait in a queue.

146
00:09:49,300 --> 00:09:54,160
The WAL archiving failing alert fired after 198 seconds.

147
00:09:54,700 --> 00:09:58,900
When the storage came back, the archive caught up within 35 seconds.

148
00:09:59,433 --> 00:10:04,013
The next incremental backup worked, and the next drill found zero missing orders.

149
00:10:05,767 --> 00:10:07,826
Disaster three is the scary one:

150
00:10:07,826 --> 00:10:08,341
bit rot.

151
00:10:08,341 --> 00:10:12,073
The test overwrites four kilobytes inside one backup file,

152
00:10:12,073 --> 00:10:16,127
keeping the same size, so nothing looks wrong from the outside.

153
00:10:16,667 --> 00:10:21,055
The nightly verification reads every file and checks its checksum:

154
00:10:21,055 --> 00:10:24,047
one invalid checksum, in exactly that backup.

155
00:10:24,600 --> 00:10:27,943
The drill fails too, as it should, and two alerts fire:

156
00:10:27,943 --> 00:10:30,800
backup verify failed, and restore drill failed.

157
00:10:31,333 --> 00:10:35,533
When the original file is put back, verification and the drill pass again.

158
00:10:35,533 --> 00:10:39,563
You learn about the damage on a normal morning, not during an incident.

159
00:10:41,300 --> 00:10:42,077
Disaster four:

160
00:10:42,077 --> 00:10:44,518
the encryption passphrase is wrong, or lost.

161
00:10:44,518 --> 00:10:49,123
Nothing can be restored, the drill fails, and the restore drill failed alert fires.

162
00:10:49,123 --> 00:10:53,174
That is exactly what you want to know before the day you need the backup.

163
00:10:53,174 --> 00:10:56,170
So keep the passphrase in a vault, outside the server.

164
00:10:56,733 --> 00:11:01,293
With the right passphrase back, the drill passes and the alert resolves.

165
00:11:01,833 --> 00:11:02,687
Disaster five:

166
00:11:02,687 --> 00:11:05,860
the database volume is deleted, as if the disk died.

167
00:11:05,860 --> 00:11:12,143
One command rebuilds the database from the backups, in sixteen seconds, with every archived order back.

168
00:11:12,700 --> 00:11:14,157
And one more safety check:

169
00:11:14,157 --> 00:11:16,734
when an empty database was started by mistake,

170
00:11:16,734 --> 00:11:20,992
the repository refused its WAL, because the system identifier did not match.

171
00:11:20,992 --> 00:11:23,850
A new database can never overwrite the old backups.

172
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:27,659
The security design is tested too.

173
00:11:27,659 --> 00:11:32,201
The database and the application cannot even resolve the storage host name.

174
00:11:32,201 --> 00:11:34,260
Only the backup host can reach it.

175
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:37,823
The raw backup files are searched for order data:

176
00:11:37,823 --> 00:11:39,550
zero readable order strings.

177
00:11:40,100 --> 00:11:46,620
The database's own pgBackRest configuration contains zero storage keys and zero passphrases.

178
00:11:47,167 --> 00:11:50,937
And the storage without credentials answers 403, forbidden.

179
00:11:52,700 --> 00:11:56,970
All of this runs in GitHub Actions, on every push and every week.

180
00:11:56,970 --> 00:11:58,349
First, static checks:

181
00:11:58,349 --> 00:12:01,436
ShellCheck, yamllint, ruff, seven pytest tests,

182
00:12:01,436 --> 00:12:05,640
hadolint for the Dockerfile, and unit tests for the alert rules.

183
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:09,049
Second, the image is scanned with Trivy.

184
00:12:09,049 --> 00:12:16,885
It found a vulnerable gosu binary in the PostgreSQL base image, with one critical and 21 high vulnerabilities.

185
00:12:16,885 --> 00:12:21,230
It was replaced with a small shim, and the scan is now clean.

186
00:12:21,767 --> 00:12:25,660
Third, the end to end test, with eleven sections:

187
00:12:25,660 --> 00:12:28,838
every disaster you just saw, in one run.

188
00:12:28,838 --> 00:12:33,367
The clean run took 898 seconds, and every section passed.

189
00:12:33,900 --> 00:12:37,772
And the same test passed in CI, on a fresh GitHub runner.

190
00:12:37,772 --> 00:12:44,497
There, the first backup took 62 seconds, the recovery brought back 108 of 108 orders in 17 seconds,

191
00:12:44,497 --> 00:12:49,320
the outage lost nothing, and the lost volume was rebuilt in 20 seconds.

192
00:12:51,067 --> 00:12:53,222
The tests found four real problems.

193
00:12:53,222 --> 00:12:57,164
The unit tests found that the WAL segment metric lost precision,

194
00:12:57,164 --> 00:13:01,537
because the timeline was shifted into bits a float cannot hold exactly.

195
00:13:02,100 --> 00:13:06,284
The alert rule tests found that one alert summary showed the wrong value,

196
00:13:06,284 --> 00:13:08,920
and another printed fractional failure counts.

197
00:13:09,467 --> 00:13:12,027
And Trivy found the vulnerable gosu binary.

198
00:13:12,567 --> 00:13:15,139
And to be honest about what was not tested:

199
00:13:15,139 --> 00:13:20,403
the lab database is about 30 megabytes, the lab uses SeaweedFS instead of real cloud S3,

200
00:13:20,403 --> 00:13:22,677
and high availability is out of scope.

201
00:13:24,433 --> 00:13:26,261
Now let us make it practical.

202
00:13:26,261 --> 00:13:29,223
You need Docker with Compose, Bash and OpenSSL.

203
00:13:29,223 --> 00:13:30,861
Git Bash on Windows works.

204
00:13:30,861 --> 00:13:33,571
And about two gigabytes of disk and memory.

205
00:13:33,571 --> 00:13:36,343
Clone the repository and go into the folder.

206
00:13:36,900 --> 00:13:43,187
Up dot S-H creates the secrets and the certificates, starts everything, and waits for the first full backup.

207
00:13:43,187 --> 00:13:44,700
That takes about a minute.

208
00:13:45,233 --> 00:13:49,757
Status shows what is in the repository and the result of the last drill.

209
00:13:49,757 --> 00:13:55,913
Drill runs a restore drill right now, and backup takes a full, differential or incremental backup.

210
00:13:56,467 --> 00:13:57,958
And now the fun part:

211
00:13:57,958 --> 00:14:02,928
drop a table yourself, and undo it with pitr dot S-H and a time stamp.

212
00:14:02,928 --> 00:14:07,757
Or delete the database volume, and rebuild it with recover lost D-B.

213
00:14:08,300 --> 00:14:12,784
Grafana runs on localhost port 3000, and Prometheus on 9090.

214
00:14:12,784 --> 00:14:19,659
The whole proof, with every disaster, is scripts slash E-2-E dot S-H, about fifteen minutes.

215
00:14:19,659 --> 00:14:22,200
And down dot S-H stops everything.

216
00:14:23,933 --> 00:14:26,807
Moving to production is a configuration change.

217
00:14:26,807 --> 00:14:29,313
Point the S3 endpoint at your AWS region,

218
00:14:29,313 --> 00:14:34,143
use port 443 and host style addressing, and remove the lab's storage container.

219
00:14:34,700 --> 00:14:40,090
Put the IAM keys into the secrets folder, or better, let pgBackRest use an instance role.

220
00:14:40,633 --> 00:14:46,153
Protect the bucket with Object Lock or versioning, so deleted or overwritten backups can be recovered.

221
00:14:46,700 --> 00:14:51,581
Keep the encryption passphrase in a password manager or a vault, outside the server.

222
00:14:51,581 --> 00:14:53,440
You saw what happens without it.

223
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,313
And route the alerts to your on call channel.

224
00:14:56,313 --> 00:15:00,220
The drill result on the dashboard is the first thing to check every morning.

225
00:15:01,967 --> 00:15:03,854
Let us be honest about the limits.

226
00:15:03,854 --> 00:15:05,686
This is a single database server.

227
00:15:05,686 --> 00:15:09,517
High availability, with replicas and failover, is a separate concern.

228
00:15:10,067 --> 00:15:12,797
The lab's object storage is one container.

229
00:15:12,797 --> 00:15:17,867
Production needs durable storage, ideally off site and versioned or immutable.

230
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:20,878
The drill restores onto the backup host.

231
00:15:20,878 --> 00:15:25,710
For very large databases, run it on a dedicated drill server with enough disk.

232
00:15:26,267 --> 00:15:27,535
And next on the list:

233
00:15:27,535 --> 00:15:31,402
a second repository in another region for three two one backups,

234
00:15:31,402 --> 00:15:37,987
Object Lock, alert routing with Alertmanager, and a Kubernetes version with CloudNativePG and the same drill.

235
00:15:39,733 --> 00:15:40,929
That is the project.

236
00:15:40,929 --> 00:15:43,797
A backup is only real once it has been restored,

237
00:15:43,797 --> 00:15:46,486
and this setup restores one every single day,

238
00:15:46,486 --> 00:15:52,103
measures how long it takes and how much data it would lose, and alerts when anything is wrong.

239
